  "Sherburne was settled in 1793 by proprietors whose descendants put up a granite monument to them at the centennial in 1893. The Register's district of 1982 holds 119 buildings in four parts: the business centre, South Main Street, William Champ Park and East State Street. It names the Sherburne Bank building (1880, later the post office), the Sanford Block (1882, now the Masonic Temple), the Sherburne Inn (1917), the Congregational Church (1864 to 1868, enlarged 1884) and the public library (1910 and 1938).",
  "The four corners of Routes 12 and 80, with the 1881 Civil War monument given by Dr. Devillo White and the 1893 settlers' monument on West Main Street. The Congregational church and the library stand together on East State Street. A 2009 photograph on Wikimedia Commons shows the Sanford Block (Masonic Temple) in red brick beside a painted bank block at the corner. The pin marks the four corners.",
